   "etymology": "An eastern Mediterranean wanderwort of unknown origin; compare Byzantine Greek πίτα (píta) (also πίττα (pítta), πήττα (pḗtta)), Bulgarian пита (pita), Serbo-Croatian pita, Turkish pita and Hebrew פיתה (pîtā) (cf. Aramaic פיתא (pītā, “piece of bread”)). Possibly from Vulgar Latin *picta (literally “painted”). However, despite Orel's reservations, the immediate source of the wanderwort is likely Greek.\nProbably not related to petë (“layer”), though folk-etymological influence is possible.",
